{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>upon the Ye'or.</b> All other rivers are not called יאורים (ye'orim) except for the Nile, because the entire land is made up of artificial channels (ye'orim) dug by human hands, and the Nile rises among them — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 41:1). But Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 41:1) translated יאור as נהרא (nahara, \"river\"); he translated only על יאוריהם (Exodus 7:19) as על אריתיהון, because there he was compelled to distinguish between them, since the verse mentions על נהרותם ועל יאוריהם (\"upon their rivers and upon their ye'orim\"). Now according to Onkelos's view, they are all called יאורים: the great ones are called נהרות (nehadrot, rivers) and יאורים (ye'orim), and those made as long excavations by human hands are also called יאורים. We find that the Tigris (חדקל) is called יאור, as it is written (Daniel 10:4), \"And I was by the side of the great river, which is the Tigris (חדקל), and behold a man clothed in linen,\" and it is written (Daniel 12:5), \"And behold two others stood, one here at the lip of the יאור and one there at the lip of the יאור, and he said to the man clothed in linen who was above the waters of the יאור.\" And in my opinion, the view of Onkelos is correct, for יאור (ye'or) and נהר (nahar) are one language, and both are from the root אורה (orah, light). And so too rain (גשם) is called אור (or, light), as it is said (Job 36:30), \"Behold, He spreads His אור upon it, and He covers the roots of the sea\" (Job 37:11), and as Rabbi Yohanan said (Genesis Rabbah 26:7): every אורה mentioned in the section of Elihu concerns the descent of rains — so the verse speaks. And perhaps because rains come about through the celestial luminaries (מאורות), and rivers are formed from them, they are traced back to their progenitors."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes two verses from Job (36:30 and 37:11) as a single combined citation; the second reference appears parenthetically after the first quotation. Also, his quotation of Job 36:30 reads יפיץ ענן אורו from Job 37:11 rather than the text of 36:30 itself, suggesting a conflation of the two verses in his source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:21.440231136Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}